---
import TreatmentPage from "../../../components/TreatmentPage.astro";

const T = "/services/fertility-treatment";

const page = {
  slug: "iui",
  name: "IUI Treatment",
  title: "IUI Treatment in Lahore | Procedure & Success Rate",
  description:
    "IUI treatment in Lahore: how intrauterine insemination works, who it suits, the step-by-step procedure, success factors, risks and cost with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  eyebrow: "IUI Treatment",
  h1: "IUI Treatment in Lahore",
  waText: "Hello, I would like to book an IUI consultation with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  ctaLabel: "Book an IUI consultation",
  heroSub: [
    "Intrauterine insemination (IUI) involves placing washed, concentrated sperm directly into the uterus through a thin catheter, giving sperm a head start toward the egg. This simple outpatient procedure is a form of “artificial” insemination: fertilization still occurs naturally inside the body.",
    "By bypassing the cervix and putting sperm close to the fallopian tubes, IUI can help when basic fertility factors (open tubes, adequate sperm, ovulation) are present.",
  ].join(" "),
  leadIn: [
    { rich: ["Because fertilization happens in vivo, IUI is generally less invasive and far cheaper than ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], ". At our Lahore clinic, Dr Kamran Saleem offers IUI to suitable couples and explains clearly when ", ["IVF/ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], " would be a better option."] },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      id: "who-is-iui-suitable-for",
      title: "Who Is IUI Suitable For?",
      icon: "user",
      body: [
        "IUI is typically offered when normal fertility “ingredients” are mostly in place but something is slightly out of sync. Common candidates include:",
        { ul: [
          "Mild male-factor infertility: such as slightly low sperm count or motility. After sperm washing, enough healthy sperm remain for IUI.",
          "Ovulation problems: irregular or absent ovulation that can be corrected with medication. Mild stimulation (letrozole, clomiphene, or low-dose injections) can induce ovulation and IUI is timed to the egg release.",
          "Cervical-factor infertility: thick or hostile cervical mucus or cervical scarring. IUI bypasses the cervix by depositing sperm directly in the uterus.",
          "Unexplained infertility: when standard tests show no clear cause, IUI is often a first-line trial before moving to IVF.",
          "Use of donor or frozen sperm: IUI is commonly used with donor or thawed sperm samples, since the sperm are washed and placed directly in the uterus.",
        ] },
        { rich: ["Conversely, IUI is not recommended when both fallopian tubes are blocked or damaged, when sperm quality is severely poor, in moderate-to-severe endometriosis, or when advanced maternal age/low ovarian reserve makes ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], " more effective. In those situations, repeating IUI is unlikely to help, and proceeding to IVF (with or without ", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], ") is usually advised. Before any IUI cycle, we ensure at least one tube is open (via HSG or sonohysterogram) and that partner sperm tests are adequate; otherwise IVF would be a better route."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "tests-before-iui",
      title: "Tests You Need Before IUI",
      icon: "document",
      body: [
        "To avoid “wasted” IUI cycles, several key fertility tests are done first:",
        { ul: [
          "Semen analysis: Confirms enough motile sperm will remain after washing. We examine volume, count, motility and morphology on a fresh sample.",
          "Tubal patency: A hysterosalpingogram (HSG) or sonohysterogram to verify at least one fallopian tube is open.",
          "Ovulation assessment: Blood tests (hormones like LH, progesterone) and serial ultrasounds to confirm you are ovulating or to monitor stimulated follicles.",
          "General health labs: Thyroid and prolactin levels (if cycles are irregular), infection screening, and a pelvic ultrasound to check the uterus and ovaries.",
          "Ultrasound/endometrial check: A scan (and sometimes saline sonogram) to ensure the uterine lining is healthy and there are no fibroids/polyps.",
        ] },
        { rich: ["If you already have recent semen, HSG or cycle monitoring results, bring them to the consultation. Otherwise, our ", ["fertility assessment", "/services/assessment"], " will cover all needed tests so we can advise whether IUI is appropriate."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "iui-procedure-step-by-step",
      title: "The IUI Procedure, Step by Step",
      icon: "calendar",
      body: [
        "Each IUI treatment cycle spans about two weeks. The actual insemination takes only a few minutes. The typical steps are:",
        { h3: "1. Cycle monitoring" },
        "Early in the cycle we do a baseline pelvic ultrasound. In a natural cycle, we use urine tests, blood hormones or ultrasound scans to track follicle growth and ovulation timing. In a medicated cycle, oral tablets (letrozole or clomiphene) or low-dose FSH injections are given to gently stimulate 1–2 follicles. We closely monitor estrogen levels and follicle size by ultrasound to know when eggs are ready.",
        { h3: "2. Ovulation trigger (if used)" },
        "When a follicle reaches maturity, an hCG injection may be given to trigger ovulation at a predictable time.",
        { h3: "3. Sperm preparation" },
        "On the day of insemination, your partner provides a fresh semen sample (or thawed donor sample). In the lab we wash the sperm: semen is centrifuged and a specialized media is added to isolate the healthiest, most motile sperm from seminal fluid. This concentrated sample greatly increases the number of active sperm available.",
        { h3: "4. Insemination" },
        "About 24–36 hours after the trigger (when ovulation is expected), the prepared sperm is placed into your uterus. You lie on the exam table; a speculum is used to open the cervix. A soft, narrow catheter is then inserted through the cervix and into the uterine cavity. The syringe attached to the catheter injects the washed sperm into the uterus. This painless procedure takes only a few minutes and requires no anesthesia. Mild cramping or spotting can occur, similar to a pap smear.",
        { h3: "5. After the procedure" },
        "You’ll rest for a few minutes and can then go home. We often prescribe progesterone support for the luteal phase. You may resume normal activities right away – no strict bed rest is needed. A home pregnancy test is taken about 14 days after insemination.",
        { rich: ["If pregnancy does not occur, most clinicians will repeat IUI for a few cycles (typically 3–4) before considering other options. We review your response after each cycle and advise when to proceed to ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], "/", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], " if further IUI is unlikely to succeed."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "iui-medication",
      title: "IUI With Ovulation Medication",
      icon: "bolt",
      body: [
        "Many IUI cycles include mild ovarian stimulation to improve success. Typically we prescribe oral medications like letrozole or clomiphene citrate, or low-dose injectable FSH. These medicines help ensure that you ovulate regularly and may produce 1–2 healthy follicles. The goal is not multiple eggs – just one or two good ones.",
        "Most IUI cycles use mild stimulation (tablets or injections) to induce one or two follicles, as illustrated above. Because fertility drugs raise the chance of multiple ovulations, we do careful monitoring with ultrasound. If too many follicles develop, the cycle may be canceled or converted (for example to IVF) to avoid high-order multiples. Overall, controlled stimulation can improve IUI success but carries small risks (see below).",
        { rich: ["Patients with irregular cycles or polycystic ovary syndrome often need an ", ["ovulation induction", `${T}/ovulation-induction`], " phase first, using similar medications, to regulate their cycle before doing IUI."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "iui-success-rates",
      title: "IUI Success Rate: What to Expect",
      icon: "heart",
      body: [
        "IUI has a modest chance of pregnancy per cycle, and the likelihood depends strongly on age and diagnosis. With good sperm and in a woman under ~35, success rates per cycle can approach 10–20%. Rates decline with age: by the early 40s, IUI chances fall sharply. Other key factors include the cause of infertility, how many motile sperm are inseminated, and how well ovulation was timed.",
        { rich: ["Most pregnancies from IUI occur in the first 3–4 cycles. If no pregnancy happens after 3–4 attempts, continuing identical IUI cycles is usually not helpful. In such cases Dr Saleem will recommend moving on to ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], " or ", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], ", which offer higher success for remaining fertility issues. During your consultation, you will get a personalized success estimate based on your tests – we do not rely on general “clinic averages” because each case is different."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "iui-risks",
      title: "IUI Risks and Side Effects",
      icon: "shield",
      body: [
        "IUI is generally a low-risk procedure, but it is not without possible downsides. Commonly reported issues are:",
        { ul: [
          "Mild cramping or spotting: It’s normal to have mild pelvic discomfort or light bleeding right after insemination. This usually settles quickly.",
          "Multiple pregnancy: Because we may use ovulation drugs, there is an increased chance of twins (or rarely triplets). We minimize this by using only mild stimulation and monitoring. Multiple pregnancies carry higher risks of preterm birth and low birth weight.",
          "Infection: The risk of pelvic infection from the catheter is very small (on the order of less than 1%). We maintain sterile technique, and antibiotics are rarely needed.",
          "Ovarian hyperstimulation (OHSS): A risk if too many follicles develop from medications. We monitor to prevent OHSS, which is uncommon with the mild protocols used.",
          "Ectopic pregnancy: Any fertility treatment carries some risk of ectopic pregnancy (egg implants in the tube). This risk is similar to natural pregnancy.",
          "Emotional stress: Repeated attempts and waiting can be emotionally challenging. We provide counseling support as needed.",
        ] },
        "Contact us promptly if you experience severe pain, heavy bleeding, fever, or any other concerning symptoms after IUI.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "iui-vs-ivf",
      title: "IUI vs IVF: How to Decide",
      icon: "compare",
      body: [
        "IUI and IVF are different treatments for different situations. In general:",
        { ul: [
          "IUI is best when the fallopian tubes are open, sperm is reasonably good, and the woman is younger. It is a first-line for unexplained infertility, mild male-factor, cervical issues or for using donor sperm. IUI is less invasive and much less expensive than IVF.",
          "IVF is best when tubes are blocked/damaged, sperm is very poor, there is moderate–severe endometriosis, or if the woman is older and time is critical. IVF (with ICSI) allows direct egg retrieval, fertilization in the lab, genetic testing of embryos, and freezing for future use.",
        ] },
        { rich: ["Choosing between IUI and IVF also depends on time and finances. Many young couples start with IUI because it’s simpler and cheaper. However, if tests suggest IUI is unlikely to succeed, starting with IVF can save time. Dr Saleem will review your case, explain the pros and cons, and may refer you to our ", ["IVF vs IUI guide", "/resources/ivf-vs-iui"], " for more detail."] },
        { rich: ["If you are considering freezing eggs or sperm before treatment, see our ", ["egg and sperm freezing", `${T}/fertility-preservation`], " page."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "iui-cost-in-lahore",
      title: "IUI Cost in Lahore: What Affects the Price",
      icon: "coin",
      body: [
        { rich: ["IUI is significantly less expensive than an ", ["IVF cycle", "/resources/ivf-treatment-in-lahore"], ", but costs can still add up. Typical price components include:"] },
        { ul: [
          "Consultation and basic tests: (semen analysis, HSG, bloodwork) often done in advance.",
          "Medications: if ovulation induction is used (letrozole or gonadotropins).",
          "Monitoring: ultrasound scans and labs to track the cycle.",
          "Laboratory fees: sperm washing/preparation.",
          "Insemination fee: the clinic visit and procedure itself.",
          "Number of cycles: since most couples need more than one IUI, the total depends on how many attempts are needed.",
        ] },
        "Because costs vary by clinic and plan, we give each patient an itemized estimate. At your first visit, Dr Saleem will outline the likely costs per cycle and discuss how many cycles are reasonable to try, avoiding unhelpful repetition.",
      ],
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: "What is IUI treatment?", a: "In IUI, a semen sample is washed and concentrated, and the best sperm are placed directly in the uterus at ovulation, to boost the chance of natural fertilization." },
    { q: "Is IUI painful?", a: "No anesthesia is needed. Most women feel only mild cramping or pressure during the a few-minute procedure, similar to a cervical screening." },
    { q: "What is the success rate of IUI?", a: "Success per cycle is modest (often under 20%) and falls with age. Younger women have higher rates. Your doctor will give a personal estimate after tests." },
    { q: "How many IUI cycles should we try?", a: "Usually 3–4 cycles are attempted, reviewed after each. If pregnancy has not occurred, moving to IVF/ICSI is typically advised." },
    { q: "When can I take a pregnancy test after IUI?", a: "About 14 days post-insemination, to avoid false positives/negatives from recent ovulation drugs." },
    { q: "Can IUI work with a low sperm count?", a: "Possibly, if it is only mildly reduced. The lab can concentrate the remaining motile sperm. With a severely low count, IVF with ICSI is usually a better option." },
    { q: "What is the difference between IUI and IVF?", a: "In IUI, sperm is placed in the uterus and fertilization happens in the body. In IVF, eggs are collected, fertilized in the lab, and one or more embryos are transferred to the uterus." },
    { q: "Do I need bed rest after IUI?", a: "No. You may lie down briefly after the procedure, but then can resume normal activity. Extended bed rest has not been shown to improve success." },
  ],
  closingHeading: "Considering IUI?",
  closingParas: [
    "IUI offers a simple, lower-cost fertility option for many couples in Lahore. Dr Kamran Saleem will conduct full ovulation monitoring, semen analysis and tubal assessment to ensure the procedure is right for you. We only recommend IUI when tests indicate a reasonable chance of success. If your results suggest IVF or ICSI would be more effective, we will advise you frankly.",
    "Bring any existing semen reports, HSG/tubal scans and cycle history to your consultation. We will review everything and plan the best path to achieving your pregnancy – whether that is IUI or another fertility treatment.",
  ],
  resources: [
    { href: "/resources/ivf-vs-iui", category: "Treatments", title: "IVF vs IUI: understanding the difference" },
    { href: "/resources/female-infertility-treatment", category: "Fertility Treatment", title: "Female Infertility Treatment: Causes, Medicines, Tests and Options" },
    { href: "/resources/male-fertility-test", category: "Male Fertility", title: "Male Fertility Test: What It Shows" },
  ],
  close: {
    heading: "Considering IUI?",
    body: "Bring your semen analysis, any HSG or tubal reports and recent cycle history to your consultation.",
    cta: "Book an IUI Consultation",
  },
};
---

<TreatmentPage {...page} />
